## Start with a purpose

A sandbox is useful for a build, a test, a coding agent or a short-lived development environment. Choose its template, workspace and resource size for the workload you intend to run.

## Understand the lifecycle

The local lab uses four visible actions: create, pause, resume and remove. Pausing keeps the simulated machine available to resume. Removing it ends that instance. A saved snapshot is a separate recovery point.

| Action | What to check |
| --- | --- |
| Create | The returned identifier and initial state |
| Execute | Exit status, stdout and stderr |
| Pause | Whether work is still running and what state is retained |
| Snapshot | The saved version and its resource scope |
| Remove | Whether required data was stored elsewhere |

## Inspect readiness

“Running” does not necessarily mean an application is ready to serve requests. Check the process, the listening port, and an application-level response. Use the platform’s readiness contract where one is available.

<Callout title="Disposable compute, deliberate persistence" kind="tip">Put data that must survive an instance into a persistent resource, or save a documented recovery point. Do not rely on a temporary filesystem by accident.</Callout>

## Try the local sandbox API

The [API explorer](/docs/api) can create and list example resources in a process-local store. It is intentionally isolated from real cloud infrastructure. Restarting the app clears these API examples.

## Practice

Open [Your first sandbox](/docs/learn/labs/sandbox) to try the lifecycle visually, then read [snapshots and persistence](/docs/snapshots).
